    </section>
    <section>
      <title>Formulas for one proportion</title>
      <slide>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <m>\pi</m> is the underlying process probability of success or population proportion of success.</li>
            <li>
              <m>n</m> is the sample size.</li>
            <li>
              <m>\hat{p} = \frac{\text{number of successes in the sample}}{n}</m> is the sample proportion.</li>
            <li>
              <m>SD = \sqrt{ \pi(1-\pi) / n } </m> is the theory-based standard deviation of the null distribution.</li>
          </ul>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <m>\text{standardized statistic} = \frac{\text{statistic} - \text{mean of the null distribution}}{\text{SD of the null distribution}} </m> is the number of standard deviations away from the mean of the sampling distribution a statistic is.</li>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <m> z = \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{\text{Simulated SD of null distribution}} </m> is the simulation-based standardized statistic for one proportion.</li>
              <li>
                <m> z = \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{SD}  =  \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{\sqrt{ \pi(1-\pi) / n } } </m> is the theory-based standardized statistic for one proportion.</li>
            </ul>
          </ul>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <m> z = \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{\text{Simulated SD of null distribution}} </m> is the simulation-based standardized statistic for one proportion.</li>
            <li>
              <m> z = \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{SD}  =  \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{\sqrt{ \pi(1-\pi) / n } } </m> is the theory-based standardized statistic for one proportion.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </slide>
    </section>
  </slideshow>
